Lab 6: Testing Camel Routes
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Create unit and integration tests for Apache Camel routes using JUnit**.
Simulate message flows and validate results using CamelTestSupport.
Test error handling and retry policies with dead-letter queues (DLQ).
Prerequisites
Basic knowledge of Apache Camel and Java.
JDK 11+ installed.
Maven installed.
An IDE (e.g., VS Code, IntelliJ IDEA).
Apache Camel dependencies (will be added in the lab).
Task 1: Setting Up the Project
Subtask 1.1: Create a Maven Project
Open a terminal and run:
mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.example -DartifactId=camel-testing-lab -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
Navigate into the project:
cd camel-testing-lab
Subtask 1.2: Add Camel Dependencies
Open pom.xml and add the following dependencies:
<dependencies>
    <!-- Camel Core -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Camel Test Support -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-test-junit5</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- JUnit 5 -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        <version>5.9.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
Save the file and run:
mvn clean install
Expected Outcome:

A Maven project with Camel and JUnit dependencies is set up.
Task 2: Writing a Simple Camel Route and Testing It
Subtask 2.1: Create a Basic Camel Route
Create a new Java class SimpleRouteBuilder.java in src/main/java/com/example:
package com.example;

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class SimpleRouteBuilder extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("direct:start")
            .log("Received message: ${body}")
            .to("mock:result");
    }
}
Subtask 2.2: Write a JUnit Test Using CamelTestSupport
Create a test class SimpleRouteTest.java in src/test/java/com/example:
package com.example;

import org.apache.camel.RoutesBuilder;
import org.apache.camel.builder.AdviceWith;
import org.apache.camel.test.junit5.CamelTestSupport;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

public class SimpleRouteTest extends CamelTestSupport {

    @Override
    protected RoutesBuilder createRouteBuilder() throws Exception {
        return new SimpleRouteBuilder();
    }

    @Test
    public void testRoute() throws Exception {
        // Mock the endpoint
        AdviceWith.adviceWith(context, "routeId", a -> {
            a.weaveByToUri("mock:result").replace().to("mock:testResult");
        });

        // Send a test message
        template.sendBody("direct:start", "Hello Camel");

        // Assert the result
        getMockEndpoint("mock:testResult").expectedMessageCount(1);
        getMockEndpoint("mock:testResult").expectedBodiesReceived("Hello Camel");
        assertMockEndpointsSatisfied();
    }
}
Expected Outcome:

The test passes, confirming the route processes the message.
Troubleshooting Tip:

If the test fails, check if routeId matches the actual route ID in SimpleRouteBuilder.
Task 3: Testing Error Handling and Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)
Subtask 3.1: Modify Route to Include Error Handling
Update SimpleRouteBuilder.java to include error handling:
public class SimpleRouteBuilder extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        errorHandler(deadLetterChannel("mock:deadLetter"));

        from("direct:start")
            .choice()
                .when(body().contains("error"))
                    .throwException(new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid message"))
                .otherwise()
                    .log("Valid message: ${body}")
                    .to("mock:result");
    }
}
Subtask 3.2: Test Error Handling in JUnit Test
Add a new test method in SimpleRouteTest.java:
@Test
public void testErrorHandling() throws Exception {
    // Send a message that triggers an error
    template.sendBody("direct:start", "error");

    // Assert the message was sent to DLQ
    getMockEndpoint("mock:deadLetter").expectedMessageCount(1);
    assertMockEndpointsSatisfied();
}
Expected Outcome:

The test confirms that error messages are routed to the dead-letter queue.
Troubleshooting Tip:

If the test fails, verify the errorHandler configuration in the route.
